### What is polymorphism and why is it useful?

The real power of polymorphism is in runtime polymorphism. It enables designing systems that are extensible without modification. Compile-time polymorphism (overloading) is convenient but does not enable extensibility.

### How does polymorphism eliminate if-else chains?

Without polymorphism, each new type requires a new branch in every switch or if-else chain.

```java
// WRONG: growing if-else chain
public java.math.BigDecimal calculateFee(String accountType, java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
    if ("SAVINGS".equals(accountType)) {
        return amount.multiply(new java.math.BigDecimal("0.001"));
    } else if ("CURRENT".equals(accountType)) {
        return amount.multiply(new java.math.BigDecimal("0.002"));
    } else if ("NRI".equals(accountType)) {
        return amount.multiply(new java.math.BigDecimal("0.003"));
    }
    // Add 10 more types -> 10 more else-if branches.
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown type: " + accountType);
}

// CORRECT: each type handles its own fee
public interface FeePolicy {
    java.math.BigDecimal calculateFee(java.math.BigDecimal amount);
}
public class SavingsFeePolicy implements FeePolicy {
    @Override public java.math.BigDecimal calculateFee(java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        return amount.multiply(new java.math.BigDecimal("0.001"));
    }
}
public class PremiumFeePolicy implements FeePolicy {
    @Override public java.math.BigDecimal calculateFee(java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        return java.math.BigDecimal.ZERO;
    }
}
// Usage: one line, no if-else.
java.math.BigDecimal fee = account.getFeePolicy().calculateFee(amount);
```

### How does polymorphism enable the Open/Closed Principle?

The Open/Closed Principle states that code should be open for extension but closed for modification. Polymorphism achieves this: add new types without changing existing code.

```java
public class PaymentProcessor {
    public void process(PaymentChannel channel, java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        channel.debit(amount);        // polymorphic call
        channel.generateReceipt();    // polymorphic call
        channel.notifyCustomer();     // polymorphic call
    }
}
// Today: NeftChannel, UpiChannel, RtgsChannel
// Tomorrow: add CbdcChannel (digital rupee) -> zero changes to PaymentProcessor.
```

### How does polymorphism power the Strategy pattern?

The Strategy pattern swaps behavior at runtime by injecting different implementations.

```java
public class AccountService {
    private final InterestStrategy interestStrategy; // injected

    public AccountService(InterestStrategy strategy) {
        this.interestStrategy = strategy;
    }

    public java.math.BigDecimal calculateInterest(Account account) {
        return interestStrategy.calculate(account);
        // Could be SimpleInterest, CompoundInterest, or TieredInterest.
        // Swap strategy via configuration. Zero code change.
    }
}
```

### How does polymorphism enable generic processing?

One loop processes all account types without type checks.

```java
public void processMonthEnd(java.util.List<Account> allAccounts) {
    for (Account account : allAccounts) {
        java.math.BigDecimal interest = account.calculateInterest();  // polymorphic
        java.math.BigDecimal fee = account.calculateFee();             // polymorphic
        account.applyMonthEnd(interest, fee);                          // polymorphic
    }
    // SavingsAccount: 4% interest, 0.1% fee
    // CurrentAccount: 0% interest, 0.2% fee
    // NRIAccount: 3.5% interest, 0.3% fee, FEMA check
    // One loop. Zero type checks. Fully extensible.
}
```

### How is compile-time polymorphism different from runtime?

| Aspect | Compile-time (overloading) | Runtime (overriding) |
|--------|--------------------------|---------------------|
| Mechanism | Different parameter lists | Same signature, child replaces parent |
| Resolved by | Compiler (argument types) | JVM (actual object type, vtable) |
| Extensibility | No: adding new form requires modifying the class | Yes: new subclass, zero changes to existing |
| Dispatch | Static | Dynamic |
| Use case | Convenience (multiple deposit forms) | Flexibility (new account types) |

### What are common mistakes with polymorphism?

The most common mistake is using instanceof checks instead of letting polymorphism do the work.

```java
// WRONG: checking type and branching
if (account instanceof SavingsAccount) {
    // savings logic
} else if (account instanceof CurrentAccount) {
    // current logic
}
// Adding NRIAccount: must add another branch. Breaks Open/Closed.

// CORRECT: let each type handle its own behavior
account.processMonthEnd(); // SavingsAccount, CurrentAccount, NRIAccount each override
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The practical test of polymorphism: can you add a new type without modifying existing code? If your design requires modifying a switch statement to add a new type, you are not using polymorphism correctly.
- In Spring, polymorphism is used everywhere: multiple implementations of an interface are registered as beans, and the correct one is injected via @Qualifier or @Primary.
- Sealed classes (Java 17) combine polymorphism with exhaustive pattern matching: the compiler ensures all subtypes are handled in a switch, and adding a new subtype causes compile errors everywhere it is not handled.
